WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:07.598
♪ When you're smiling
When you're smiling ♪

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.440
- Oh, shit!
- I SAID OVER EASY!

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:16.780
- Now, why did I do that?

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.860
[Music]

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.180
When people evaluate great actors,
what often gets mentioned most is range:

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.480
how many different characters they can play;

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:30.420
how many different emotions
they can realistically, movingly show.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:34.880
But I think a criterion
that's just as telling as range is depth:

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:39.140
how many different ways an actor
can portray the same emotion.

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.660
I spent the last few weeks watching
pretty much every film

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.260
Jack Nicholson ever made.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.000
And it's clear that the man can do it all,

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.640
but it's also no mistake that
intensity is his calling card.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.840
Today I'd like to explore the ways that
Jack Nicholson conveys

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:55.780
and uses anger in his work;

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:57.420
the angles he approaches it from,

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.434
the spins and emphases he puts on it

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.360
in order to see what makes
Jack Nicholson so great.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:10.480
[Screaming]

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.700
Anger is an emotional response,

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:15.500
a reaction to something.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.320
That reaction manifests psychologically
but also...

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:19.380
physically.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.440
Everyone's had that sensation of being...

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.320
convulsed with anger.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:26.093
What Nicholson shows
again and again in his roles

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:29.900
is how anger can literally
possess the body like a spirit.

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:32.080
- Because I really want to know.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:35.700
Because if it's a mistake,
maybe we can do something about it.

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:38.860
This is the moment when
anger becomes aggression.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:41.700
Half the time, that aggression
in Nicholson's work

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:45.220
is realized as actual violence
against someone else

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:48.760
whether it's psychotic violence
or in some way...

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:49.700
justified.

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:55.360
The other half of the time, this
theatrical anger takes place

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.120
in Nicholson's own personal space.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:58.480
He flails around—

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.100
sometimes as a threat or
a suggestion of violence to come.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:06.260
A lot of the time, it's just a release—
a discharge of pent-up energy.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.020
To me, these moments

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:11.140
(the weird contortions and spasms
that Nicholson makes)

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:12.860
are more poignant than

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:14.440
actual attacks on people.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:16.700
They're a kind of exploration of...

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:19.180
the relationship between body and mind.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.880
And it never feels like overacting.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:27.240
I think that's because these gesticulations
are all unique to the particular anger

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:28.540
that they spring from;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.900
whether it's focused, pointed anger
or generally frustrated anger

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:34.880
or petulant childlike anger.

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:36.620
- You see that sign, sir?

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:37.980
Yes, y'all have to leave.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.940
I'm not taking any more
of your smartness and sarcasm.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:42.820
- You see this sign?!

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:45.540
For Nicholson

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:47.040
—and everybody else, for that matter—

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:49.740
anger can be a form of desperation,

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:53.600
a noise so loud that you don't
have to hear your own insecurities.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:55.960
In Mike Nichols' "Carnal Knowledge" for example,

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:58.420
Nicholson plays a playboy
who objectifies women

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:00.000
and refuses to commit.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.220
One of his lovers asks him to get married

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:03.440
and he...explodes.

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:06.520
- Where the fuck is my shoehorn?
This place is a mess!

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:07.980
There's not any food in the house.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:09.880
Half the time, you look like
you fell out of bed.

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:14.060
Nicholson acts a kind of anger here
that it's easy to see right through.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:15.460
The larger and louder it is,

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:18.960
the closer he is to recognizing
a vulnerability in himself.

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:20.800
- You want an extra $50 a week?

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:22.220
TRY vacuuming!

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:23.640
You want an extra hundred?

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:25.460
MAKE THIS GODDAMN BED!

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:27.380
TRY OPENING SOME GODDAMN WINDOWS.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:29.480
That's why you can't stand up in here.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.020
THE GODDAMN PLACE SMELLS LIKE A COFFIN.

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:34.540
That's the challenge for an actor
playing this emotion.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.820
You're not just playing anger;

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.480
you're playing what's under it.

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:40.700
Most anger isn't psychotic.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:43.500
It's only a thin veneer
for what's brewing below,

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:45.840
and you have to be able
to turn up the volume

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:48.600
while preserving traces
of this deeper motivation.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:50.620
That's what Nicholson excels at.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:53.700
In "The Border", for example,
Nicholson plays a border agent

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.860
who gets caught up with
his partner's human smuggling operation.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:00.180
When the partner starts killing his competition,
Nicholson realizes that

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:01.920
he's gotten in too far.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:03.780
The key emotion in this exchange

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:04.900
isn't anger

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:05.900
but fear.

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:07.580
Fear at what he's gotten himself into.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:09.840
Fear that he won't be able to get himself out.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.400
Nicholson bursts with anger here.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.690
But it's in the silent moments
where the actual feeling lies.

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:17.489
- I don't care about your fucken money.

00:04:17.489 --> 00:04:18.400
- But you took it...

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:20.920
- Your fucken life is gonna be fly shit
before I ever get involved 'n murder.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:22.960
You understand what I'm saying to you?

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:24.220
Do ya?

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:27.140
This is why Nicholson's characters
seem real in their anger.

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:29.300
Even with great writing
and great direction,

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:31.760
it's only the actor who can make a character...

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:32.700
polyphonic;

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:35.120
like more than one thing
is happening at the same time.

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:38.100
And even when you do that,
the particular expression of anger

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:41.080
has to rise out of
the particular character's personality.

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:41.940
For example,

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:45.360
take these two examples of anger
which are both underlaid

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:47.780
with a strong sense of superiority.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:49.720
- You can't handle the truth.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:52.780
Son, we live in a world that has walls,

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:55.900
and those walls have to be guarded
by men with guns.

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:57.080
Who's going to do it?

00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:57.980
You?

00:04:57.980 --> 00:04:59.300
You Lieutenant Weinberg?

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:01.580
- Never!
Never...

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:03.780
interrupt me.
okay?

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:05.440
Not if there's a fire.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:08.240
Not even if you hear
the sound of a thud from my home

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:09.480
and one week later...

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:13.700
there's a smell coming from there
that can only be a decaying human body.

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:15.720
And you have to hold a hankie
to your face

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:19.020
because the stench is so thick
that you think you're going to faint.

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:21.060
Both of these men feel smarter

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:23.400
and more important than
the person they're lecturing to.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.159
But the first is a lifelong military man

00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:27.400
in a court of law.

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:29.780
Nicholson barely moves his body or head.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:33.060
Every swivel of the neck
is measured and exact.

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:35.580
He's so restrained that
a simple lean forward

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:36.600
feels like...

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:37.480
he's lost it.

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:39.500
- I did the job
- DID YOU ORDER THE CODE RED?

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:41.120
- YOU'RE GODDAMN RIGHT I DID!

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:44.660
The second man is smug
and in his own space.

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:47.180
His movements are freer, looser.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:50.520
And they even seem to mock 
Greg Kinnear's homosexuality.

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:52.680
- Don't knock!

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:54.220
Not on this door.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:57.600
Not for ANY reason.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:01.380
Do you get me, sweetheart?

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:04.120
No two expressions of anger are the same;

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:06.720
not in real life and not in Nicholson's work.

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:08.780
Sometimes his anger is comedic,

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:10.700
sometimes...cartoonish.

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:12.820
Sometimes it's quiet.

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:16.000
Sometimes it's misplaced and sometimes

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:18.700
it's deeply, deeply wounded.

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:19.680
- Freddy,

00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:21.820
whatever you're doing stop it.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:25.680
- Freddy, whatever you're doing stop it.

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:29.460
- I hope you die.

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:33.580
- I hope you fucking die.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:35.991
Watching so much of Nicholson's acting,

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:38.920
you start to get a sense
of the larger shape of anger

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:40.500
as a human phenomenon;

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:44.840
its extremes, its physicality
and its dangers, but also its...

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:47.100
necessity in the course of a life.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:49.220
None of us can avoid anger.

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:52.520
You can't remove it
from the constellation of feelings.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:54.440
But you can understand it.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:07:00.600
Jack Nicholson has a wide range of talents,
as wide a range as an actor as anybody else.

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:06.620
And yet if all he ever did
was give us these sixty years of insights

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:10.640
into anger,
it would still be a career well spent.

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:16.700
[Music]

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:18.900
Hey, everybody.
Thanks for watching.

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:21.640
That was a lot of
Jack Nicholson movies to see.

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:24.420
But it was totally worth it.
You learn a lot by watching a master.

